# apps/web — environment
#
# Copy to `.env.local` for development. `.env.local` is never committed.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE RULE: NEXT_PUBLIC_ MEANS PUBLIC.
#
# Next.js inlines every NEXT_PUBLIC_* value into the JavaScript bundle that is
# shipped to the browser. Anyone can read it with view-source. No secret — no
# API key, token, database URL, signing key or password — may ever carry the
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefix, whatever the convenience. If a value must stay secret it
# is read on the server only, from an unprefixed variable, and never imported
# into a client component. Every variable in this file is public by design.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# The canonical origin of this deployment. Required.
#
# Read once in src/lib/site.ts as SITE_URL, and from there it becomes
# metadata.metadataBase, every canonical tag, every Open Graph URL, the absolute
# URLs in sitemap.xml, and the sitemap and host lines in robots.txt.
#
# It is a variable and not a literal on purpose: an earlier repo pinned the
# production domain into metadataBase, so every preview deployment emitted
# canonicals pointing at production and competed with the pages it was
# previewing. Set this per environment.
#
# No trailing slash. A bare hostname is accepted and gets https:// prefixed.
# Unset, it falls back to http://localhost:3000 — correct in development, and
# obviously wrong in production, which is the failure a missing value should
# produce rather than a silently wrong domain.
#
#   development  http://localhost:3000
#   preview      https://<the preview deployment's own URL>
#   production   https://<the real domain>
NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=http://localhost:3000


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# ADVERTISING. Four variables, all unset. See MONETISATION.md for the runbook.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# THE SWITCH. One of off | reserve | on. Unset means "off" in production and
# "reserve" in development.
#
#   off      A mapped ad placement renders NOTHING — no box, no label, no
#            reserved height, no element. No loader, no ad request, no
#            third-party origin contacted. This is what production ships today
#            and what must stay true until AdSense approves the site: a
#            reviewer who finds a reserved empty box labelled "Advertisement"
#            is looking at an ad that failed to load.
#
#   reserve  Every mapped placement paints its reserved, labelled box and
#            loads nothing. This is how the placement map is inspected and how
#            the CLS measurements in MONETISATION.md were taken. Safe on a
#            preview origin. NEVER set it on production before approval.
#
#   on       Post-approval. Boxes render AND the loader is mounted — inside
#            <ConsentGate>, so it cannot execute before an explicit accept.
#
# A misspelled value throws at build time rather than falling back to "off": a
# switch the operator believes is on and is not costs a week.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ADS_MODE=off


# THE NETWORK. Three values, and they are deliberately NOT written into the
# source. `ADSENSE-AUDIT.md` P32 records that grepping this repository for the
# ad network's script host finds comments and never code; a constant in a
# source file becomes a string in a deployed JavaScript chunk whether or not
# any page references it, and "we do not ship the loader" is a much better
# sentence to be able to say during review. So the vendor lives in
# configuration, and the exact values to paste are in MONETISATION.md,
# "Flipping the switch". All three are public by construction.
#
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_ADSENSE_CLIENT   the publisher ID, ca-pub-…
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_AD_LOADER_SRC    the full https URL of the loader script
#   NEXT_PUBLIC_AD_UNIT_TAG      the identifier used as both the ad element's
#                                class and the global queue it pushes onto
#
# Setting these WITHOUT setting NEXT_PUBLIC_ADS_MODE=on does nothing at all —
# every function that reads them refuses to run unless the switch is on.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_ADSENSE_CLIENT=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AD_LOADER_SRC=
# NEXT_PUBLIC_AD_UNIT_TAG=

# IMPORTANT: NEXT_PUBLIC_* variables are inlined at BUILD time, not read at
# runtime. Setting this only on the running server has no effect — canonicals,
# robots.txt and sitemap.xml will all emit localhost. It must be present in the
# build environment (Vercel: set it for the Production environment before the
# build, not as a runtime variable).
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SITE_URL=
